# =============================================================================
# Line-ending and binary handling
# =============================================================================
# This repository is developed on Windows and built inside Linux containers.
# Without these rules Git's per-machine core.autocrlf setting decides how each
# file is stored, so the same file can arrive in the index differently
# depending on who staged it - producing diffs that are entirely line endings.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text files and normalise them to LF in the
# repository, while still checking them out with native endings on Windows.
# The explicit `eol=lf` entries below are files that MUST be LF regardless of
# platform, because they are consumed by Linux tooling where CRLF breaks them.
# =============================================================================

* text=auto

# Consumed by Linux shells, container builds and infrastructure tooling. A CRLF
# here is a real failure, not a cosmetic one: `/bin/sh` reads the trailing \r as
# part of the command and the script dies with an obscure "not found".
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.tf text eol=lf
*.tfvars text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts, which require CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never normalise: line-ending substitution corrupts binary content.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.onnx binary
*.pth binary
*.pt binary
*.mp4 binary
*.avi binary
*.webm binary
